  "analysis": "Market regime: broad recent weakness — 9 of 15 assets are down over the last 20 sessions (A08 -6.1%, A05 -6.3%, A15 -5.9%, A10/A09 -4.8%, A14 -5.0%), and the portfolio sits in a -3.37% drawdown with an 'alert' flag. No news feed is connected. Since forecast medians carry no directional skill, I anchor decisions on interval width (the reliable risk signal), cross-asset redundancy, and the drawdown context. The tightest 5-day 80% intervals are A02 (0.0389), A09 (0.0428), A06 (0.0433), A04 (0.0484) and A11 (0.0541); the widest are A01 (0.1063), A14 (0.0965), A07 (0.0941), A05 (0.0891) and A13 (0.0884). The current book is already broadly aligned with that risk ordering, so I make only two real trades: (1) add ~2.5pp to A02, the single lowest-uncertainty asset with the lowest equity volatility (11% ann.) and near-flat 20d return (-0.12%) while everything else sold off — core US exposure at the tightest forecast interval; (2) trim A04 from 13.0% to 11.0%, reducing the largest single-position concentration back toward its 7.7% baseline while keeping health exposure via A07. I keep cash at 20% (vs 10% baseline and 20.9% current): with a drawdown alert and no reliable directional signal, elevated cash plus the tight-interval bond ballast (A09 at 11%) is the prudent posture. Wide-interval, weak-momentum names (A10, A12, A03, A05, A15, A14) stay near zero — A10 is redundant with A02 at a wider interval and worse momentum. All group exposures remain well under the 30% cap (us_broad 16.5%, tech_growth 11%, health 14%), every ETF is under 25%, every stock under 10%, no leverage, weights sum to 1 with cash >= 10%. Overall confidence is modest (0.55): the tilts rest on reliable width information and drawdown prudence, not on predicted direction.",
